\b How To Open This App On Another Computer\b0\par
\par
\b 1. Install the tools\b0\par
Install:\par
- Node.js\par
- VS Code\par
\par
Optional for Android testing:\par
- Android Studio\par
\par
\b 2. Get the project files\b0\par
Use one of these options:\par
\par
\b Option A: GitHub\b0\par
Clone the repo:\par
\par
git clone https://github.com/paulaboza21/globalsportsid.git\par
\par
\b Option B: USB backup\b0\par
Copy this folder to the other computer:\par
\par
C:\Global\GlobalSportsID-FullBackup\par
\par
\b 3. Open the project in VS Code\b0\par
Open the app folder in VS Code.\par
\par
\b 4. Make sure .env exists\b0\par
- If you used the USB backup, .env should already be there.\par
- If you used GitHub, create a .env file and add your real Supabase values.\par
\par
You can use .env.example as the guide.\par
\par
\b 5. Install packages\b0\par
Open the terminal in VS Code and run:\par
\par
npm install\par
\par
\b 6. Start the app\b0\par
Run:\par
\par
npx expo start -c\par
\par
\b 7. Open the app\b0\par
After Expo starts:\par
- Press w for web\par
- Press a for Android emulator\par
\par
\b 8. Edit the app\b0\par
Edit the files normally in VS Code and save your changes.\par
\par
\b 9. Save your new changes\b0\par
Run:\par
\par
git add .\par
git commit -m "backup"\par
git push\par
\par
\b Notes\b0\par
- Keep .env private.\par
- Do not upload .env publicly.\par
- Keep both GitHub and USB backups updated.\par
}
